        "title": "Covering indexes and index-only scans",
        "body": "A normal secondary index lookup is two steps. The database walks the B+tree to the leaf entries for the key, which hold the indexed columns and a pointer to the row (the primary key in InnoDB, a tuple id in Postgres), then follows the pointer into the table to fetch the other columns. That second step is a random read for every matching row, and for a query returning thousands of rows it dominates the cost.\n\nA **covering index** contains every column the query needs, so the second step is skipped. The plan shows it as an index-only scan (Postgres) or \"Using index\" (MySQL). The query `SELECT email FROM users WHERE country = 'IN' AND created_at > '2026-01-01'` is covered by an index on `(country, created_at, email)`: the first two columns are used to seek and filter, and the last one is carried in the leaf just to be returned. In SQL Server and recent Postgres versions you can add non-key columns with `INCLUDE (email)`, which stores them in the leaf without making them part of the sort order.",
